    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d72">

                <pb facs="0180" n="72" sameAs="frus:frus1893/pg72"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Thompson to Mr. Gresham.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States.</hi><lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Petropolis</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1893-11-10"><hi rendition="#italic">November 10, 1893</hi></date>.
                        (Received December 18.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 70.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that the U. S.
                    S. <hi rendition="#italic">Newark</hi> arrived at Rio de Janeiro October 19 and
                    on the 20th I had the pleasure of a call from Rear-Admiral Stanton at the U. S.
                    consulate, which I returned on Saturday the 21st and was received with the usual
                    formalities and great kindness by the admiral on board the <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Newark.</hi></p>
                <p>On the 22d I had occasion to visit the <hi rendition="#italic">Newarh</hi> again
                    to consult the <pb facs="0181" n="73" xml:id="pg73"/> Admiral, and before my
                    departure Rear-Admiral de Mello, commander of the revolting squadron, came on
                    board. When the visitor was announced I signified my desire to leave, but it was
                    not convenient for me to do so at once, and a few minutes later I was introduced
                    by Admiral Stanton to the commander of the insurgent naval forces. His visit was
                    brief and seemed to be entirely of a social character, as no allusion whatever
                    was made to politics or the revolution.</p>
                <p>In this connection it may not be out of place for me to state in justice to that
                    gentleman that during his stay here and my official intercourse with him I saw
                    nothing whatever to indicate partiality on the part of Admiral Stanton.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Thos. L.
                    Thompson.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
